\addlinespace
Mistral-7B ($T{=}0.3$) & FOLIO & 0.53 & 1.77 & 58.33 & $-$1.47 \\
Mistral-7B & LogiQA & 0.51 & 1.81 & 51.00 & $+$0.50 \\
Mistral-7B (s456) & FOLIO & 0.51 & 1.82 & 55.88 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B & FOLIO & 0.50 & 1.84 & 55.88 & $+$3.43 \\
Mistral-7B (s123) & FOLIO & 0.49 & 1.88 & 54.41 & $+$2.94 \\
Mistral-7B (s789) & FOLIO & 0.41 & 2.17 & 54.41 & $-$0.49 \\
Mistral-7B ($T{=}0.5$) & FOLIO & 0.52 & 1.80 & 58.33 & $-$0.98 \\
Mistral-7B (multi-T) & FOLIO & 0.48 & 1.91 & 57.84 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B ($T{=}1.0$) & FOLIO & 0.48 & 1.91 & 55.39 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B & PW-D5 & 0.21 & 3.62 & 39.33 & $\mathbf{-2.83}${*} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\subsection{Symbolic Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:confirmation-bias}

The trace correlation above manifests through a specific mechanism that explains \emph{all three} regime failures: self-extracted constraints systematically confirm the model's majority answer regardless of correctness. On Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$), when SICA errs, 83.3\% of extracted constraints support the incorrect majority answer---a bias ratio of $4.97\times$ (raw constraint counts; $4.24\times$ when MaxSAT-weighted) compared to correct problems. Consequently, 94\% of SICA errors overlap with SC errors: symbolic aggregation adds no independent corrective signal.

The causal chain is: the model generates an answer, then extracts FOL consistent with it. Constraints from majority-answer traces use trace-specific variable names (56.4\% of cross-answer pairs share zero Z3 variables), so MAX-SAT retains nearly all, and aggregation reduces to a weighted majority vote (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}). Confirmation bias generalizes across model families: bias ratios of $4.97\times$ (Mistral-7B), $3.93\times$ (Qwen2.5-14B), and $2.88\times$ (LLaMA-3.1-8B) all significantly exceed $1.0$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}). The bias ratio gradient aligns with regime boundaries: high bias (Mistral, $4.24\times$ weighted) at intermediate SC produces SICA's only positive trends; low bias (LLaMA, $2.66\times$) at high SC produces the worst degradation ($-4.41$\,pp), because low confirmation bias at high SC means the model's consensus is already near-correct and constraints provide no leverage. The oracle ceiling (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) confirms that ${\sim}$88\% of the same-model potential gain is lost to self-extraction quality.


\subsection{Process-Level Independence}
\label{sec:process-kappa}

Answer-level $\kappa$ may underestimate the independence bottleneck: two traces can follow nearly identical reasoning yet reach different final answers, causing $\kappa$ to treat them as disagreeing. We define process similarity as the mean pairwise cosine similarity of TF-IDF trace representations:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:process-sim}
\text{ProcessSim}(q) = \frac{2}{K(K{-}1)} \sum_{i < j} \cos(\mathbf{v}_i^q, \mathbf{v}_j^q),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{v}_i^q$ is the TF-IDF vector of trace $t_i$ for question $q$. On Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$), mean ProcessSim is $0.69$, far exceeding answer-level $\kappa = 0.41$. The process--answer correlation is model-strength dependent: $r = -0.06$ ($p = 0.36$) on Mistral-7B, where answer diversity does not reflect reasoning diversity (Figure~\ref{fig:process-kappa}), rising to $r = +0.24$ ($p < 0.001$) on Qwen2.5-14B. This pattern generalizes across four families (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}): the cosine--$\kappa$ gap narrows from $0.23$ (Mistral) to $0.02$ (Qwen-14B), meaning answer-level $\kappa$ already captures the bottleneck for strong models, while process-$\kappa$ reveals hidden homogeneity in weaker models where answer diversity masks process convergence. In all families, interventions targeting answer diversity (temperature scaling, diverse prompting) cannot alter the underlying reasoning paths. TF-IDF cosine similarity captures lexical overlap rather than semantic equivalence, making it a lower bound on true process similarity; semantic embedding methods would likely yield even higher values.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_process_kappa_scatter.pdf}
\vspace{-2mm}
\caption{Answer agreement vs.\ CoT cosine similarity (Mistral-7B, FOLIO, $n{=}204$). Flat regression ($r = -0.06$, $p = 0.36$); stronger models show significant positive correlation ($r = +0.24$, Qwen-14B; Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}).}
\label{fig:process-kappa}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Supporting Diagnostics}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

Further diagnostics---constraint ablation, discriminativeness, entropy decomposition, and hyperparameter sensitivity---confirm the mechanism (Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}).


\subsection{Remediation Analysis}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

We test more than twenty remediation strategies spanning prompt engineering, method-level redesign, and ten additional directions. All produce neutral or negative results.

\paragraph{Prompt-level interventions.} Nine strategies spanning six dimensions (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:remediation}) all fail. Contrastive prompting reduces non-discriminative constraints from 8.2\% to 6.0\%, but newly discriminative constraints still mirror the majority ($\Delta = -1.1$\,pp). Cross-model extraction fails in both directions: weaker models yield 0\% discriminative constraints; stronger models produce accuracy within $\pm 1$\,pp of self-extraction. Multi-temperature sampling ($T \in \{0.3, 0.7, 1.2\}$) yields $\Delta = 0.00$\,pp.

\paragraph{Method-level interventions.} ICG ($\Delta \approx 0$, $p = .206$), Contrastive ($\Delta = -9.31$\,pp, $p < 0.01$), and Debate ($\Delta = -18.63$/$-23.00$\,pp, $p < 0.001$) all fail; three of six conditions reach significance, all negative (Table~\ref{tab:method_interventions}, Appendix~\ref{app:remediation}).

\paragraph{Additional directions.} Ten further experiments (code verification, iterative refinement, logprob filtering, cross-model ensembles, alternative objectives) all produce neutral-to-negative results (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:additional_interventions}). Cross-model SICA exemplifies the pattern: reducing $\kappa$ from 0.53 to 0.42 yields $\Delta = -0.49$\,pp---cross-architecture diversity is insufficient within self-extraction.

\paragraph{Independence-accuracy trade-off.} Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization} compares Fleiss' $\kappa$ across four same-model aggregation methods and three model families (Figure~\ref{fig:kappa-accuracy}). SC ($\kappa = 0.44$), Diverse-Prompt ($\kappa = 0.40$), and ICG ($\kappa = 0.47$) cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$; Debate reaches $\kappa = 0.15$ but collapses accuracy by $-18.6$\,pp. All three families cluster above $\kappa = 0.40$ (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model-kappa}), forming a same-model trade-off ceiling at the 7B--32B scale tested: methods that increase independence destroy accuracy, and methods preserving accuracy cannot break the correlation floor.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Independence-accuracy trade-off (FOLIO, $n = 204$). Top: aggregation methods on Mistral-7B; bottom: SC across three model families. All families produce $\kappa \geq 0.41$, confirming an architecture-independent ceiling. $\kappa$: Fleiss' kappa on raw answers.}
\label{tab:kappa_generalization}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccrr@{}}
\toprule
Model & Aggregation & $\kappa$ & Eff-$K$ & $K$ & Acc.\ (\%) \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & Debate & 0.15 & 4.44 & 12 & 38.24 \\
& Diverse-Prompt & 0.40 & 2.21 & 12 & 54.41 \\
& Self-Consistency & 0.44 & 2.02 & 10 & 56.86 \\
& ICG & 0.47 & 1.93 & 10 & 51.96 \\
\addlinespace
LLaMA-3.1-8B & Self-Consistency & 0.41 & 2.17 & 12 & 66.18 \\
Qwen2.5-7B & Self-Consistency & 0.66 & 1.46 & 12 & 75.98 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & Self-Consistency & 0.74 & 1.32 & 12 & 75.49 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Breaking Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:solutions}

The analyses in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}--\S\ref{sec:remediation} identify two structural causes of SICA's failure: constraints are coupled to their source answer (confirmation bias), and all traces share the same model distribution (independence bottleneck). We investigate two complementary approaches: decoupling constraints from answers within the same model (\S\ref{sec:decoupling}), and injecting an architecturally independent verification signal (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}).

\subsection{Constraint-Answer Decoupling}
\label{sec:decoupling}

Symbolic confirmation bias arises because the model generates an answer, then extracts constraints consistent with it (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). The causal chain runs through the trace conclusion: the final answer anchors extraction toward confirming that specific answer.

We break this chain by truncating the conclusion from each reasoning trace before constraint extraction. The final answer segment (${\sim}$4.1\% of trace text) is removed so the extraction model sees only intermediate reasoning steps, not the concluded answer. All other pipeline components---deduplication, MAX-SAT solving, scoring---remain identical to standard SICA (\S\ref{sec:pipeline}).

On Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$), truncated-SICA shows a declining trajectory as evaluation progresses: $\Delta = +11.3$\,pp at $n = 70$ (early, easy-problem bias) $\to$ $+3.1$\,pp at $n = 120$ $\to$ $+1.3$\,pp at $n = 140$ $\to$ $+0.6$\,pp at $n = 180$. The near-complete result ($+0.6$\,pp, n.s.) indicates that removing the answer segment is insufficient: the intermediate reasoning steps themselves carry enough answer signal to reconstruct confirmation bias during extraction.

\subsection{Cross-Architecture NLI Verification}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

The oracle gap (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) shows that the MAX-SAT mechanism is sound given quality constraints; twenty-plus same-model strategies fail to provide them (\S\ref{sec:remediation}). The corrective signal must come from outside the reasoning model's distribution.

\paragraph{Method.}
We test this with DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M parameters, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}. For each problem, DeBERTa classifies the (premise, conclusion) pair into \textsc{entailment}, \textsc{contradiction}, or \textsc{neutral}. In combo mode, the NLI verdict is integrated with SC's $K$ votes via a weight parameter $w$: each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$).

\paragraph{Main result.}
DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\%, below SC's 54.41\% baseline. Combo mode at $w{=}1$ (NLI as 13th voter) reaches 57.84\% ($\Delta = +3.43$\,pp, McNemar exact $p = 0.039$; discordant pairs: $b{=}1$, $c{=}8$; 95\% bootstrap CI $[0.59, 6.28]$\,pp). The effect increases with weight: $w{=}3$ yields 58.82\% ($+4.41$\,pp, $p = 0.078$, n.s.; $b{=}6$, $c{=}15$; CI $[0.00, 8.82]$\,pp) and $w{=}5$ reaches 60.29\% ($+5.88$\,pp); neither reaches significance. Higher weights risk dataset-specific overfitting; we report $w{=}1$ as the primary result. Note that this cross-architecture approach operates outside the same-model self-extraction scope of SICA (\S\ref{sec:pipeline}), serving as a proof-of-concept for external signal integration.

\paragraph{Per-label complementarity.}
The gain is not uniform across labels. On \textsc{False} problems, NLI achieves 71.43\% compared to SC's 38.10\% ($+33$\,pp), because DeBERTa's contradiction detection directly identifies negated conclusions that the LLM's chain-of-thought reasoning systematically mishandles. On \textsc{Unknown} problems, the pattern reverses: SC reaches 68.12\% while NLI drops to 18.84\% ($+49$\,pp for SC), because ``neutral'' in NLI does not map cleanly to the logical \textsc{Unknown} label. The combo succeeds because these strengths are complementary. When both systems agree (45.1\% of problems), accuracy reaches 68.48\% ($+14.07$\,pp over SC), confirming that agreement between architecturally independent systems serves as a high-precision signal.

\paragraph{Why cross-architecture verification works.}
The 406M encoder-only model and the 7B decoder-only LLM share no architecture, training data, or inference mechanism. DeBERTa classifies textual entailment rather than generating chain-of-thought, so it does not exhibit the symbolic confirmation bias identified in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}. The two systems err on different problem subsets, satisfying the independence condition that same-model sampling cannot provide.

